      <h1>Conductance vs Load Test</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> The <strong>conductance tester</strong> sends a small AC signal and
        estimates CCA from how well the battery conducts — <strong>fast, non-destructive</strong>, works on a
        partially charged battery. The <strong>load test</strong> draws a real current (about half CCA for 15
        seconds) and watches the voltage — <strong>slower, more demanding, definitive</strong>. Conductance
        screens; the load test verdicts.
      </div>

      <h2>The comparison</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Conductance</th><th>Load test</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Signal</strong></td><td>Small AC injection</td><td>Real DC load (½ CCA, 15 s)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Speed</strong></td><td>Seconds</td><td>~15+ seconds plus setup</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Battery stress</strong></td><td>None</td><td>Real — needs decent charge first</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Answer</strong></td><td>Estimated CCA (health proxy)</td><td>Direct delivery verdict</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Screening, partial charge</td><td>Final verdict, warranty</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>When each wins</h2>
      <p>
        Conductance wins where <strong>speed and gentleness</strong> matter — fleet screening, batteries at any
        state of charge, the modern shop floor. The load test wins where the <strong>verdict must be final</strong> —
        warranty decisions and disputed diagnoses — because it measures the actual delivery, not a correlate
        (see <a href="/load-testing/">load testing</a> and
        <a href="/resistance-conductance/">conductance</a>). They agree on healthy batteries; the disagreement
        cases are where the load test's directness matters.
      </p>
      <h2>When the two disagree</h2>
      <p>
        The disagreement case is the interesting one: a battery with high conductance but a failing load test
        usually has a <strong>delivery problem the AC signal cannot see</strong> — a bad internal connection or a
        plate issue that only shows under real current. The rule: when the screen and the verdict split, the
        load test wins, because it measured the actual delivery the application needs.
      </p>


      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Where I stand:</strong> Conductance vs load test is proxy vs ground truth — the electronic tester is
        the better screening tool, the load test is the better court — and a serious diagnosis uses the fast tool
        first and the direct one when the answer matters.</p>
        <p><strong>The reasoning:</strong> The AC tester correlates with delivery; the load test is delivery. When they
        agree, either suffices; when they disagree, the direct measurement wins. Knowing which question is being
        asked — screen or verdict — is how the two tools are used correctly.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

